{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghCoFJn3eJG534qCpoTX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Eye of the Bolt-Holder** is built: a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler inspired by Eye of the Beholder. The full test command passes: 1,241 checks across 19 suites, 0 failures. That includes a scripted playthrough of the whole game and the AI Hub harness.\n\n- **Game:** `EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-185046.html` (1.95 MB) in the root. It opens from disk; three.js and Rapier load from jsDelivr, so it needs a connection.\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` in the root parses and every field is filled.\n- **Dev folder:** same name, with the sources, the patched engines, the tests, `README.md`, `PATCHES.md` and `CONTENT.md`.\n\n**What's in it**\n- Four floors and the Rat Run race floor, with every puzzle type the plan lists:\n  - plates weighed down by rocks, levers, timed buttons and loose stones;\n  - secret and illusory walls, pits and trapdoors, silent teleporters and a spinner;\n  - candles lit in order, three hidden deeds and the three-ward Undergate.\n- A party of up to six. Each hand acts on its own cooldown, Party Strike uses everyone ready, and the formation has three rows.\n- 18 spells memorized or prayed at camp, plus scrolls and wands. Camp also covers rest (which can be interrupted), food and saves.\n- 15 monster types plus the boss. Every blow is wound up visibly, so you can step aside from it.\n- The Bolt-Holder fight: turn three Great Locks, dodge lightning behind pillars, and survive a relock and a phase where it calls more monsters.\n- Three recruitable NPCs with talking portraits and babble voices; Veil's choice changes floor 3.\n- Intro and ending cutscenes, a title, party creation, the automap, a journal, a game-over screen, credits and an About screen.\n- **Controls:** the 10-foot UI works with a pad, keyboard or mouse. Prompts follow the last device, with PlayStation glyphs by default and an Xbox option. Touch-only devices get a notice instead of touch controls.\n- **Tools and multiplayer:** Dev Mode (live sliders with a reset, cheats, warps, spawning, overlays) and a 9-scene attract mode. The AI Hub race supports 1–4 players and passes the harness at 2 players, at 4 players with lag and packet loss, and when the host leaves. On its own, the Rat Run is a solo time trial against your best ghost.\n\n**Limits I found**\n- Under software rendering in headless Chromium the 3D floors run at about 3 frames a second. Performance was out of scope, and I haven't seen it on a real GPU.\n- I haven't tested a physical controller; the pad was checked through the input system's button codes.\n- The walkthrough bot wins 18 of 20 random seeds; its two losses are at the Bolt-Holder, which is a real fight.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:50:46\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - Genre: real-time, grid-based party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles.\n  - Perspective: first person at the party's eyes on a 4-way grid, with tweened steps and quarter turns; a top-down parchment automap; full-screen overlay screens.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the 2D engine from `dist/handoff` (patched).\n  - my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (patched), on three.js r182 (WebGPU, falling back to WebGL 2) and Rapier 0.19.3 from jsDelivr.\n  - AI Hub session client 1.4.0.\n  - Plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium 1243) for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native retro-modern look:\n  - code-drawn low-resolution 3D (pixel picture, toon light, outlines, fog, torch shadows);\n  - rigs drawn as pixel-art cards;\n  - pen-drawn creatures, mechanisms and item models;\n  - a brass \"lockwork\" UI with pixel-art item icons.\n- **Animation:**\n  - The engine's procedural rigs and moves (E.Humanoid, E.Blob, E.MOVES and E.Attack wind-ups).\n  - A tweened first-person camera: steps, turns, bumps, falls and head bob.\n  - Pen-animated creatures (leech, rat knot, brass spider, hound, gazer, the Bolt-Holder) and mechanisms (levers, gates, gears, Great Locks, the Undergate's bolts).\n  - First-person weapon swings and card animation.\n- **Audio:** All in code:\n  - original step-sequencer songs (title, four floors, boss, camp, ending, race) with fight layers and stings;\n  - about 90 synthesized sound effects, panned by bearing, plus floor ambience;\n  - a WebAudio formant babble synth for Animalese/Simlish-style voices, one profile per speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode, 1–4 players. The Rat Run race has the game's own card, others shown as ghosts in seat colours, progress reports and a timed result. Offline, a solo Rat Run against your best run's golden ghost.\n- **Testing:** Any headless testing was allowed. I ran 19 suites behind one command (`tests/run.mjs`, printing only failures and a count):\n  - content lint and reachability;\n  - the sim: movement, features, items, combat, monsters, magic, camp, progress, save, determinism and babble;\n  - UI, Dev Mode, attract mode and an AI Hub stub, all through `game.advance` and `game.debug`;\n  - a full bot playthrough on Normal, both of Veil's branches, also run over 20 seeds;\n  - Playwright browser checks from `file://`;\n  - the AI Hub harness: 2 players, 4 players with latency, jitter and loss, and the host leaving;\n  - screenshots at 1280×720 and 1024×768.\n- **Research:** The plan's own research on the source, the engine and the AI Hub protocol. During the build I only read the engine's docs and source and the hub README; no web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown (tested in headless Chromium 1243)\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** API cost unknown; token counts unknown; session time about 2 hours (18:50 to about 20:50, from file timestamps).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 19 real issues:\n   - 2 content errors from the lint.\n   - 3 bot and balance problems from the playthrough. The worst: the party collapsed at the boss in 8 of 20 seeds until the boss and the bot were tuned.\n   - 1 rest rule: a waiting level-up was lost when a rest ended early.\n   - 4 from the browser checks:\n     - a key tapped and released within one frame was ignored;\n     - keys not mapped to any action didn't end the attract mode;\n     - a crash entering play with null scene data;\n     - the race clock ran during its countdown.\n   - 6 look and text problems: characters the pixel font lacks, overlapping text, a dark parchment, and similar.\n   - 2 from the hub harness: \"Ready\" was lost while the 3D was still loading; WebGPU errors inside the harness's frames.\n   - 1 engine warning: the floors ran out of light slots.\n2. **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 4:\n   - the ignored key taps;\n   - the lost \"Ready\" on the hub's card, which would have kept the game out of hub sessions;\n   - the party collapsing at the boss;\n   - the null-data crash.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The full scripted playthrough. The bot plays every floor and the boss by the real rules in under 2 seconds of test time. Running it over 20 seeds exposed the boss balance.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The engine's `tools/check.mjs`. It confirmed there were no errors, but its view switching and look notes are written for 2D views and gave nothing a fixed-camera 3D game could act on.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** Everything is drawn by code and runs deterministically:\n   - the pen and THINGS system built a whole bestiary, every mechanism and about 50 item models with no asset files;\n   - the rigs drawn as cards gave every character a live look and portrait;\n   - exact fixed steps made the whole game testable headless.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - wall colours come from the legend entry, not the level's colour setting;\n   - `input.hold` takes an action, not a key code;\n   - a scene's data can be null, which a default parameter didn't catch;\n   - a tap released before the next step reads as pressed but not held;\n   - the 3D runs at about 3 fps under software rendering, so tests had to wait on game state rather than the clock.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The crawler as a pure, deterministic sim:\n   - the grid party, per-hand combat and telegraphed monster minds;\n   - spells, camp and food;\n   - a data-driven wiring language for every puzzle;\n   - the walkthrough bot that plays it through the same commands as a player.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Mixing its HTML pause, settings and save screens with the game's canvas-drawn screens, and checking the 3D look only through slow software rendering.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n   - 2D engine:\n     - beards and shut eyes for E.Humanoid;\n     - PlayStation and Xbox as the only button symbols, PlayStation first;\n     - a voice bus with its own volume;\n     - panned sound effects;\n     - Options ignored while Create is held (the hub's rule);\n     - a way to hide Load in a hub session.\n   - 3D engine: `world.wallBlock`, which builds blocks from the level's own wall materials so secret and illusory walls match the walls around them.\n   - Each change is listed in `PATCHES.md`.\n6. **If you could change one thing, what would it be?** Let a 3D level's cells change at run time (a wall that opens, floor that becomes a pit), instead of baking the level once.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** It's complete and testable: exact deliverables, a detailed plan to build from, clear testing rules, and an exit interview in a fixed shape the About screen can share.\n2. **What was confusing?**\n   - The rule to brainstorm a title and pick it with a random number, when the plan had already fixed the title.\n   - Whether Online required multiplayer in a genre that is single-player at heart. The plan answered it with a ghost race.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting every system the plan lists into one build, and still checking the 3D look under software rendering.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Say how long a first play of the demo should be and which systems may be cut if time runs short, and say whether GPU rendering is available for screenshot checks."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":736,"cache_read_input_tokens":897882,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":736},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d562a4a4-1880-473a-a05f-38e08e0d0050","uuid":"85569c71-72f0-4f8e-b098-b143640b71a7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:48:54.279Z","request_id":"req_011CghCoEwxWWHLJfUQrqH1Y"}
