{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<system-reminder>\n<tools-used-before-summary>Bash, Edit, Read, ScheduleWakeup, TaskStop, ToolSearch, Write</tools-used-before-summary>\n</system-reminder>\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - The user's /goal reads: \"Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.\"\n   - STAGE.md: build the game TASK.md describes, following PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort) and using the title it gives; TASK.md wins where they disagree; deliver the file name and the reply sections.\n   - TASK.md asks for:\n     - an original game inspired by Eye of the Beholder 1991, with genre and perspective chosen;\n     - a title riffed from the source and picked with a real RNG (the plan did this: `secrets.randbelow(50)` → index 5, \"Eye of the Bolt-Holder\");\n     - a single HTML file named `EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-185046.html` in the root, with dev files in a subfolder of that name; CDN dependencies are OK;\n     - built on the my-3d2dge handoff (the engine may be changed), using the engine's native art and animation as the base;\n     - all audio in code with Animalese/Simlish voices;\n     - AI Hub multiplayer, and the game on its own;\n     - a feature-complete demo;\n     - a dev mode from the pause menu with live sliders and a reset to defaults;\n     - an attract mode after 10 s idle on the title/menu, which any input exits;\n     - a writing tone like the source's;\n     - a 10-foot, controller-first UI: PS5 DualSense standard mapping, native keyboard and mouse (hover moves focus), prompts that follow the last input, PlayStation glyphs with a Settings option for Xbox;\n     - no dense web screens;\n     - touch-only devices: no touch controls, a \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice plus the settings button, and the attract mode still runs;\n     - no TODOs, placeholders or stubs;\n     - Source Information, Testing Feedback (4 questions), Engine Feedback (6) and Prompt Feedback (4) in the chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen;\n     - exit-interview.json in an exact shape.\n   - Performance is unlimited and FPS is out of scope.\n   - Testing: headless scripts, including a scripted playthrough, behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; screenshots only for what scripts can't check.\n   - Security/process constraints, verbatim from TASK.md:\n     - \"Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine's ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\"\n     - \"Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\"\n   - /tmp is a shared, nearly full tmpfs. I set `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` for Playwright.\n   - Git: commit only when asked. Commits end with \"Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\". No commits were made.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Engines:\n     - my-3D2dge v0.85.2 2D engine: E.Game, scenes, input actions/codes (Pad0–Pad15, Mouse0/2), settings, saves, E.Humanoid/E.Blob rigs, E.font, E.ui, px.\n     - The 3D engine (E.World3D): pen, THINGS, world.mob, world.light, world.glow, world.arc, world.project, fixed camera, overlay pen.\n     - three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3 from jsDelivr through an importmap.\n   - Game architecture: a deterministic sim (B.S state, mulberry32 RNG streams, commands → events) separate from the Presenter (View3D) / NullView, audio and UI.\n   - Wiring language: triggers and actions as data. The walkthrough bot drives the playthrough, the attract mode and Dev auto-play through commands.\n   - AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode:\n     - session.js is inlined first in `<head>`, with an early `window.__hubInfo = AIHub.connect(...)`;\n     - the game shows its own card, sends at ≤10 Hz unreliable, reports progress about once a second, sends one result, handles time-up, leave and end;\n     - the harness passes when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true.\n   - Testing tools: a Node vm loader of the engine and sources; `game.advance`/`game.debug`; Playwright 1.63 with Chromium on SwiftShader (`?backend=webgl`). The 3D runs at about 3 fps, so browser tests wait on game state.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections (dev folder `…/work/EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-185046/`):\n   - `src/` sources, concatenated in sorted order into one IIFE by `tools/build.mjs`:\n     - Core and data:\n       - `00-core.js`: B, DX/DY, TAU, rnd/dice, the B.TUNE slider table (22 rows), B.CHEATS, B.DIFF, B.stepTimes, B.opt.\n       - `01-util.js`: helpers.\n       - `10-data-items.js`, `11-data-monsters.js`, `12-data-spells.js`: items, monsters, spells.\n       - `13-data-classes.js`: classes, races, premade party, NPCs, PEOPLE.\n       - `14-data-text.js`: B.TXT, BARKS, STORY, INTRO, ENDING.\n       - `15-data-floors.js`: f1–f4 and race; maps, doors with `lk: 'simple'|'warded'`, feats, wires, items, mons, walk.\n       - `16-data-about.js`: B.Q and B.ABOUT, the source of truth for About and exit-interview.json.\n     - Sim:\n       - `20-sim-state.js`: newGame, mkMember, initFloor (boltholder → B.makeBoss), serialize/load/hash, demoParty.\n       - `21-sim-map.js`: idx, passability, FOV.\n       - `22-sim-wiring.js`: say, hint, trigger, act, talk.\n       - `23-sim-party.js`: movement, arrival, transit, plates, doors, body.\n       - `24-sim-items.js`: inventory, useAhead, locks, alcoves, examine.\n       - `25-sim-combat.js`: hands, strike, projectiles, damage.\n       - `26-sim-monsters.js`: AI.\n       - `27-sim-magic.js`: spells.\n       - `28-sim-camp.js`: camp, progress, joinNpc, dismiss.\n       - `30-sim-boss.js`: boss phases; BOSS_INT [3.5, 3.0, 2.5]; slam 2d6.\n       - `31-sim.js`: command and B.update; race functions.\n     - Presentation and audio:\n       - `40-view3d.js`: View3D build/step/draw; lights 11; eye light 9 power.\n       - `41-view-things.js`: THINGS and item models.\n       - `42-view-creatures.js`: CREATURES, PEN_BODIES, drawEye.\n       - `43-view-fx.js`: events → fx, hands, hotspots; NullView.\n       - `50-audio.js`: SFX, SONGS, the Sound director with ambience, Voices babble synth.\n     - Input and UI:\n       - `60-input.js`: KEYS (mL: KeyQ, mR: KeyE), Controls (dn() = down||pressed), Idle (counts any keydown via B._keyAt).\n       - `61-ui-kit.js`: panel, parch, glyph/badgeOf (with 'lr'/'ud'), rich, icons, Focus (noClick/noClickSlots).\n       - `62-ui-hud.js`: UI stack, drawHud, contextPrompts (hidden during talk), drawTransit, level-up toast.\n       - `63-ui-party.js`, `64-ui-magic.js`, `65-ui-camp.js`, `66-ui-map.js` (map/journal/reader), `67-ui-dialog.js` (TalkBox/PlayTalk).\n       - `68-ui-shell.js`: title, create, about, over, credits; B.aboutPages; touchOnly.\n     - Tools, multiplayer and scenes:\n       - `70-dev.js`: Dev Mode, 9 tabs.\n       - `71-attract.js`: 9 vignettes; `input(allowMove = true)`, so keys exit from the first frame and pointer movement only after 0.3 s.\n       - `72-bot.js`: Bot (formation rotation, ward front row, potions earlier on f4; boss step timeout 420 s).\n       - `73-hub.js`: Hub; the keydown Enter → answer('ready') listener during the card; gamepad poll; startRace; hubcard; raceResults.\n       - `80-cutscenes.js`: stages A/B/D and the cutscene scene.\n       - `90-main.js`:\n         - the game, GAME_SETTINGS, saves, B.cmd (runs a command now and dispatches its events), Play.dispatch;\n         - scenes start/attract/play: play.enter guards null data with `d = d || {}`; the race world waits while locked;\n         - BOOT, game.debug, a window keydown counter `B._keyAt`.\n   - `engine/my-3d2dge.js` patches:\n     - beard/eyesShut rigs;\n     - glyphs setting (playstation default, playstation/xbox options);\n     - voice bus with `bus()` and voiceVolume;\n     - sfx pan;\n     - Options ignored while Pad8 is held;\n     - `saves.canLoad`;\n     - a reworded version-stamp comment (removed the word \"placeholder\").\n   - `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` patch: `world.wallBlock` (plus `L._sideMat`/`L._topMat`).\n   - `tools/build.mjs`: bundles session.js, metas, importmap, engines and game into the root HTML; writes `../exit-interview.json` from B.ABOUT; reports empty fields.\n   - `tests/`:\n     - `run.mjs`: one command; `--fast`, `--verbose`. ORDER = task, lint, movement, features, items, combat, monsters, magic, camp, progress, save, determinism, babble, ui, dev, attract, hub, playthrough, browser, gamepad, harness. BROWSER = browser, gamepad, harness.\n     - Helpers: `util.mjs` (fresh, sim, at), `load.mjs`, `shot.mjs`, `shoot.mjs`, `png.mjs`.\n     - `suites/*.mjs`, 21 suites, including:\n       - `task.mjs`: parses TASK.md itself — bracket name, exit-interview template, Source Information fields, feedback questions, Dev Mode, attract, glyphs, keys, touch.\n       - `lint.mjs`: also scans the whole bundled page and the exit interview for TODO, FIXME, placeholder, stub and audio files.\n       - `gamepad.mjs`: a fake DualSense through `navigator.getGamepads`.\n       - `harness.mjs`: node:http server on 127.0.0.1:0; `game=game.html?backend=webgl`.\n   - Docs: `README.md`, `PATCHES.md`, `CONTENT.md`, `TASK-COVERAGE.md` (requirement → built → proven by; points to `node tests/run.mjs task --verbose`), and `TESTING-ISSUES.md` (20 issues with fixes; 4 critical).\n   - Root outputs:\n     - `EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-185046.html` (about 2.05 MB);\n     - `exit-interview.json`: parses, every field answered, aiHub genre RPG, players \"1–4\", 6 tags.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - **Stray `cat > /tmp/null` hang:** stopped my own task with TaskStop.\n   - **Door option `kind` overwrote the door kind:** renamed the option to `lk`.\n   - **Bot and test harness:**\n     - The bot's `place` step never finished; the boss step timed out; boss wipes. Fixed by tuning the boss (slam 2d6, intervals) and the bot (formation rotation, ward, potions). Now all 20 seeds win.\n     - The test helper name `run` collided with the suites' exported `run`; renamed it to `sim`.\n     - Several test setup errors were fixed in the tests themselves.\n   - **Sim:** a pending level-up was lost when a rest ended early; it now applies when everyone is full.\n   - **Font and look:**\n     - Characters the font lacks (… · — ✓ █) drew as \"?\"; replaced them and sanitized E.versionLabel.\n     - The log overlapped the row labels; the parchment came out dark (new `parch()`); prompts showed under the dialogue.\n     - The title camera sat inside the party; the ending framing was too close; the skip label overlapped the corner buttons; the eye glare was too strong; floor 3 was too dark; the party-screen header overlapped.\n   - **Browser:**\n     - Quick key taps were dropped (Controls now uses pressed as well as down).\n     - Unbound keys didn't count as input (window keydown counter).\n     - play.enter crashed on null data.\n     - The race clock and monsters ran during the countdown.\n   - **Harness:** Enter was lost during boot (a keydown listener now answers Ready); WebGPU errors in iframes (the harness now loads with `?backend=webgl`).\n   - **Engine warning:** light slots exhausted; floors now have 11.\n   - **Attract-mode key in its first 0.3 s was ignored:** the guard now applies to pointer movement only. Found by the task suite.\n   - **Stop hook feedback (repeated):** demanded transcript evidence for every TASK.md feature and no TODOs or stubs. In response I:\n     - re-read TASK.md and STAGE.md in the transcript;\n     - added the task suite and the whole-bundle lint scan;\n     - added the gamepad suite;\n     - wrote TASK-COVERAGE.md and TESTING-ISSUES.md;\n     - printed the verbose task-suite checks (61 passed).\n     - The hook still objects that the checks assert presence rather than function, that the coverage file came from memory, and that there was no physical controller or GPU.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - All test results are green.\n     - Last full run: 1,309 passed, 20 suites.\n     - After later changes: Node `--fast` 1,282 passed (18 suites); browser, gamepad and harness 46 passed.\n     - 20/20 playthrough seeds win.\n   - Ongoing: providing transcript evidence that the TASK.md features work, grouped per requirement from the behavioral checks (the dev suite tests that sliders change systems, the attract suite tests start and exit, the hub and harness suites, the playthrough, the UI flows, gamepad behavior).\n   - A physical controller and a real GPU are unavailable. State this honestly; the simulated pad goes through the browser API.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - The /goal command (quoted in section 1), plus the system reminder that the Stop hook enforces it.\n   - \"Check the full test run result in tests/out/full.log and finish the deliverables.\" (a /loop-style prompt from ScheduleWakeup)\n   - Stop hook feedback 1: the transcript doesn't establish every TASK.md feature or the TODO scan beyond src; no per-issue record for the ~19 issues.\n   - Stop hook feedback 2: the transcript never shows TASK.md/STAGE.md being read; TASK-COVERAGE.md was written from memory; 18/20 seeds; software rendering.\n   - Stop hook feedback 3: the task suite was self-written, checks were run only in the last round, the coverage file was from memory, software rendering, a simulated pad.\n   - Stop hook feedback 4 (latest): \"'every feature in TASK.md is implemented' is not shown by transcript evidence. The 'task' suite ... asserts presence or wording of features (e.g. 'the keyboard uses WASD...', 'Dev Mode has live sliders'), not that each feature works. TASK-COVERAGE.md was composed from memory of the plan, not from a line-by-line checked list. The 3D look was only checked via screenshots under software rendering, and no physical controller was tested (a simulated pad was used).\"\n   - The final instruction: respond with text only, a summary.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Finish the evidence pass:\n     - when the background verbose run (task bep4cx8ch → `tests/out/verbose.log`) completes, generate a TASK-EVIDENCE file grouping the passing behavioral check names by TASK.md requirement;\n     - print the condensed evidence in the transcript;\n     - possibly regenerate TASK-COVERAGE.md from it.\n   - Give the final reply again with all four sections (Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback), stating honestly that no physical controller or GPU was available.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   - Immediately before the summary I responded to the latest Stop hook feedback by launching:\n     `cd …/EyeOfTheBoltHolder-…-185046 && timeout 1700 node tests/run.mjs --verbose > tests/out/verbose.log 2>&1; tail -1 tests/out/verbose.log` (background, task id bep4cx8ch).\n   - Stated intent: \"I'll rerun everything in verbose mode, build an evidence file grouped by TASK.md requirement from the passing check names, and print it here.\"\n   - `--verbose` prints `ok   <suite>: <check>` for every check. Behavioral examples already in the suites:\n     - dev: \"Monster HP x2 doubles a new rat's HP\", \"Reset to defaults restores every number\", \"Party damage x4 hits about four times as hard\", each cheat and warp;\n     - attract: \"10 s idle: the attract mode\", \"every vignette plays\", \"a key/pad/mouse/tap exits\", \"no saves or dev storage written\";\n     - hub and harness: the full round;\n     - playthrough: the campaign is won, deeds and secrets;\n     - ui: flows;\n     - gamepad: the 19 pad behaviors;\n     - browser: boot, walk/fight, attract, phone notice, settings button.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   - Wait for task bep4cx8ch to finish.\n   - Read `tests/out/verbose.log` and generate a `TASK-EVIDENCE.md` (or update TASK-COVERAGE.md) mapping each TASK.md requirement (Package, Working Environment, Tech Stack, Art/Animation, Audio, Online/Multiplayer, Testing, Scope/feature-complete, Dev Mode, Attract Mode, Narrative tone, UI/UX controller/keyboard/mouse/glyphs, dense screens, Touch-only, Done, Source Information/About, Exit interview) to the specific passing behavioral checks.\n   - Print the condensed list in the transcript.\n   - Rebuild only if src changed.\n   - Then give the final reply with the four required sections, honestly noting that a physical controller and a GPU were unavailable (a simulated standard-mapping DualSense went through the browser gamepad API; the 3D was checked by screenshots under software rendering, and performance is out of scope per TASK.md).\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build--20261103-185046--x53986f-work/d562a4a4-1880-473a-a05f-38e08e0d0050.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar